      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Given that  $f(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t,\\,7t⟩,$   what is ${f}^{′}(\\pi )?$\n\n${f}^{′}(\\pi )=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1,\\,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(\\pi ),$ we substitute $t=\\pi $ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-1,\\,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4,\\,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
